const CHAPTERS = [
  { year: -2300, from: -2450, view: 'gulf', title: 'The first cities trade',
    text: 'Sumer, Egypt and the Indus cities were already linked. Lapis lazuli from Badakhshan reached Ur and Egypt, and Sargon of Akkad boasted that ships from Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha moored at his capital.' },
  { year: -1350, from: -1400, view: 'eastmed', title: 'The great powers\' club',
    text: 'Egypt, Hatti, Mitanni, Babylon and Assyria wrote to each other as "brothers" in the Amarna letters, exchanging gold, horses and princesses. Ships like the one wrecked at Uluburun carried Cypriot copper and tin.' },
  { year: -1180, from: -1280, view: 'eastmed', title: 'The Bronze Age system breaks',
    text: 'Within a few decades of 1200 BC the Hittite state fell, Ugarit and the Mycenaean palaces burned and Egypt fought off the Sea Peoples. Long-distance trade shrank, and writing disappeared from Greece for some four centuries.' },
  { year: -500, from: -560, view: 'persia', title: 'One empire, Aegean to Indus',
    text: 'The Achaemenid Persian Empire was the largest state yet. The Royal Road ran about 2,700 km from Sardis to Susa, and royal couriers covered it in about a week. Lydia had recently struck the first coins.' },
  { year: -324, from: -336, view: 'persia', title: 'Greek speakers reach the Indus',
    text: 'Between 334 and 323 BC Alexander\'s army marched from Macedon to the Punjab. His empire split at his death, but Greek kings ruled Bactria for over a century and Greek styles shaped the Buddhist art of Gandhara.' },
  { year: -126, from: -140, view: 'silk', title: 'Han China looks west',
    text: 'Emperor Wu sent Zhang Qian west to find allies against the Xiongnu. He found none, but his reports of Ferghana\'s horses and Bactria\'s markets led the Han to push garrisons along the Hexi Corridor. This is usually taken as the start of the Silk Roads.' },
  { year: 100, from: 60, view: 'silk', title: 'Rome and Han at either end',
    text: 'Four empires spanned Eurasia: Rome, Parthia, Kushan and Han. Few people travelled the whole way; goods passed hand to hand. Monsoon winds carried ships from Roman Egypt to India each year, and in 97 the Han envoy Gan Ying reached the Persian Gulf and turned back.' },
  { year: 700, from: 600, view: 'world', title: 'Beyond Eurasia',
    text: 'Elsewhere, other worlds grew on their own. Maya cities had writing and a precise calendar, Tiwanaku and Wari ruled the Andes, Ghana grew rich on Saharan gold, and Polynesian navigators had settled Tonga and Samoa. The Americas developed apart from Eurasia.' },
  { year: 755, from: 740, view: 'silk', title: 'Tang and Abbasids meet at Talas',
    text: 'In 751 Tang and Abbasid armies met near the Talas River. The Tang lost, and after the An Lushan rebellion of 755 they pulled back from Central Asia. A later story says Chinese prisoners taught papermaking in Samarkand; paper mills appear in Baghdad by the 790s.' },
  { year: 1287, from: 1200, view: 'silk', title: 'Pax Mongolica',
    text: 'The Mongol conquests made the largest land empire in history. By 1287 it had split into four khanates, but travel across them was easier than ever: Marco Polo went from Venice to Kublai Khan\'s court, and Rabban Bar Sauma went the other way, from Beijing to Rome and Paris.' },
  { year: 1352, from: 1336, view: 'silk', title: 'The Black Death',
    text: 'The same routes carried plague. It reached the Black Sea at Caffa in 1346, then rode Genoese ships to Constantinople, Sicily and Marseille. Within five years it killed perhaps a third to a half of Europe\'s people, and similar shares in Egypt and Syria.' },
  { year: 1498, from: 1400, view: 'world', title: 'Ships overtake caravans',
    text: 'Ming fleets reached East Africa between 1405 and 1433, then stopped. Portuguese ships rounded Africa and reached Calicut in 1498, six years after Columbus crossed the Atlantic. Sea routes would soon carry more than the caravan roads, and the Americas were joined to the rest of the world.' },
];

